[{:db/id 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a47f5f1d-1328-52e8-8c90-bfca32946e28" "question-6fc12b03-7934-57bc-b6a6-b1ef9ff5ade7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179672 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4ab286f6-317f-53cd-ac02-bf3eb20fe604" "question-6da1b103-36df-5031-b2ef-46cb571416a8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8399ab15-70cf-5db8-b1d1-61088de736be" "question-3412530f-81b3-51bb-95a9-4599a55eb207"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a47f5f1d-1328-52e8-8c90-bfca32946e28" :question/id #uuid "a47f5f1d-1328-52e8-8c90-bfca32946e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-125419" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1a4c61a4-7435-599c-afcf-0efe6b1cbdb6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1a4c61a4-7435-599c-afcf-0efe6b1cbdb6" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a4c61a4-7435-599c-afcf-0efe6b1cbdb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f546e017-61e1-549c-83d5-ab9a05185633" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b1e72ea2-4855-5111-a3dd-cd04fb8b2403" "answer-990826b9-b6bc-588a-80e6-36af02d8f2ee" "answer-f546e017-61e1-549c-83d5-ab9a05185633" "answer-3124c0f4-4c6e-55ec-9349-8032b33b382f" "answer-e80ae482-54b0-51f8-b405-74f535b48c44"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1e72ea2-4855-5111-a3dd-cd04fb8b2403" :answer/id #uuid "b1e72ea2-4855-5111-a3dd-cd04fb8b2403" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-990826b9-b6bc-588a-80e6-36af02d8f2ee" :answer/id #uuid "990826b9-b6bc-588a-80e6-36af02d8f2ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f546e017-61e1-549c-83d5-ab9a05185633" :answer/id #uuid "f546e017-61e1-549c-83d5-ab9a05185633" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3124c0f4-4c6e-55ec-9349-8032b33b382f" :answer/id #uuid "3124c0f4-4c6e-55ec-9349-8032b33b382f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e80ae482-54b0-51f8-b405-74f535b48c44" :answer/id #uuid "e80ae482-54b0-51f8-b405-74f535b48c44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6fc12b03-7934-57bc-b6a6-b1ef9ff5ade7" :question/id #uuid "6fc12b03-7934-57bc-b6a6-b1ef9ff5ade7" :question/math-academy-id "q-125425" :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{C}(y-z)\\,\\text{d}x+6{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-4y\\,\\text{d}z$ is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+R\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P=y-z,Q=6{z}^{2},R=-4y.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}((y-z)\\,i+6{z}^{2}\\,j-4y\\,k)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(y-z)\\,\\text{d}x+6{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-4y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $F=(y-z)\\,i+6{z}^{2}\\,j-4y\\,k.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f968e9bb-1698-556c-94c3-47e9ed5d0dfa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f968e9bb-1698-556c-94c3-47e9ed5d0dfa" :answer-field/id #uuid "f968e9bb-1698-556c-94c3-47e9ed5d0dfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6829492d-3f92-5206-8c0f-7015db0fd5bb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6829492d-3f92-5206-8c0f-7015db0fd5bb" "answer-49a222b7-ced0-5334-a476-927827cacddd" "answer-44901697-c22c-53ba-bb65-14f1081533bb" "answer-d7e04aa2-786d-5004-9af7-6898c9098f86" "answer-ecda143d-5ef3-59db-8104-b35b72d657b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6829492d-3f92-5206-8c0f-7015db0fd5bb" :answer/id #uuid "6829492d-3f92-5206-8c0f-7015db0fd5bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y-z)\\,i+6{z}^{2}\\,j-4y\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49a222b7-ced0-5334-a476-927827cacddd" :answer/id #uuid "49a222b7-ced0-5334-a476-927827cacddd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y-z)x\\,i+2{z}^{3}\\,j-2{y}^{2}\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44901697-c22c-53ba-bb65-14f1081533bb" :answer/id #uuid "44901697-c22c-53ba-bb65-14f1081533bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12z\\,j-4\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7e04aa2-786d-5004-9af7-6898c9098f86" :answer/id #uuid "d7e04aa2-786d-5004-9af7-6898c9098f86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}∇(6{z}^{2}-z-2y)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecda143d-5ef3-59db-8104-b35b72d657b0" :answer/id #uuid "ecda143d-5ef3-59db-8104-b35b72d657b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∇(6{z}^{2}-z-2y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4ab286f6-317f-53cd-ac02-bf3eb20fe604" :question/id #uuid "4ab286f6-317f-53cd-ac02-bf3eb20fe604" :question/math-academy-id "q-77148" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c3b1312e7368d8fc760a274a49280b10d4571a06ee6237b28fc8a8b1b7316d3.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $DEFD,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {-{C}_{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{3}$ is the same path as ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-16807e0b-25b3-557d-839e-c6e7045de982"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-16807e0b-25b3-557d-839e-c6e7045de982" :answer-field/id #uuid "16807e0b-25b3-557d-839e-c6e7045de982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9fd91543-0a88-52f5-89ca-61af0000467f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-57f5be3d-e9a5-5b71-9237-641c57905868" "answer-8d876fc2-4110-59e7-a123-efe4ad754f58" "answer-9fd91543-0a88-52f5-89ca-61af0000467f" "answer-ba144e5b-e62a-51ae-8288-ad68474c5bd4" "answer-6f4582a3-9b84-5136-bbc8-4a5b81e6b276"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57f5be3d-e9a5-5b71-9237-641c57905868" :answer/id #uuid "57f5be3d-e9a5-5b71-9237-641c57905868" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d876fc2-4110-59e7-a123-efe4ad754f58" :answer/id #uuid "8d876fc2-4110-59e7-a123-efe4ad754f58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fd91543-0a88-52f5-89ca-61af0000467f" :answer/id #uuid "9fd91543-0a88-52f5-89ca-61af0000467f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba144e5b-e62a-51ae-8288-ad68474c5bd4" :answer/id #uuid "ba144e5b-e62a-51ae-8288-ad68474c5bd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f4582a3-9b84-5136-bbc8-4a5b81e6b276" :answer/id #uuid "6f4582a3-9b84-5136-bbc8-4a5b81e6b276" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6da1b103-36df-5031-b2ef-46cb571416a8" :question/id #uuid "6da1b103-36df-5031-b2ef-46cb571416a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-77134" :question/problem "![](images/74/746a05b2345e50173ed898b741944ba435d17a44dfc075848be9d8fcd2f2ad90.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $PQRP,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={-{C}_{1}}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{1}$ is the same path as ${C}_{1}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c4c4c60-58a2-5309-91fd-f009939a8cdc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c4c4c60-58a2-5309-91fd-f009939a8cdc" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c4c4c60-58a2-5309-91fd-f009939a8cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46aa7018-0782-59bf-8f3c-c481140a5532" :answer-field/choices ["answer-46aa7018-0782-59bf-8f3c-c481140a5532" "answer-d3414a07-8bc9-5a0b-8216-6eb6ba5bc40b" "answer-e26dd6a1-e2c4-5310-904f-4261a100d96e" "answer-f3ecc9b3-f68e-5629-8e61-d1c693d7d7ad" "answer-248f19de-9914-5bab-a8d3-9da16c44f98d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46aa7018-0782-59bf-8f3c-c481140a5532" :answer/id #uuid "46aa7018-0782-59bf-8f3c-c481140a5532" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3414a07-8bc9-5a0b-8216-6eb6ba5bc40b" :answer/id #uuid "d3414a07-8bc9-5a0b-8216-6eb6ba5bc40b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e26dd6a1-e2c4-5310-904f-4261a100d96e" :answer/id #uuid "e26dd6a1-e2c4-5310-904f-4261a100d96e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3ecc9b3-f68e-5629-8e61-d1c693d7d7ad" :answer/id #uuid "f3ecc9b3-f68e-5629-8e61-d1c693d7d7ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-248f19de-9914-5bab-a8d3-9da16c44f98d" :answer/id #uuid "248f19de-9914-5bab-a8d3-9da16c44f98d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8399ab15-70cf-5db8-b1d1-61088de736be" :question/id #uuid "8399ab15-70cf-5db8-b1d1-61088de736be" :question/math-academy-id "q-77244" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4ce481ac8babb7a3e5707f1ef44417b12e73abe4b4c2143c19b45332642c6d2.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment  from the point $B(0,8)$ to the point $A(-2,8),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a section of the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ from the point $A(-2,8)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the section of the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ from $A$ to $O.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $-2\\le t\\le 0.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=-{t}^{3}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =xy\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =-{t}^{4}\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{3})j \\\\  & =i-3{t}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(-{t}^{4}\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j)\\cdot (i-3{t}^{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =-{t}^{4}+3{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =16+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{t}^{4}+3{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16+{[-\\frac{1}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{6}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =16+[0-(-\\frac{(-{2}^{5})}{5}+\\frac{(-2)^{6}}{2})] \\\\  & =16-\\frac{192}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{112}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b523b1e1-5cbd-5efb-b1af-a137b9b5cd25"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b523b1e1-5cbd-5efb-b1af-a137b9b5cd25" :answer-field/id #uuid "b523b1e1-5cbd-5efb-b1af-a137b9b5cd25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7527daea-f713-54e2-9b27-ffd3fc3bb110" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7527daea-f713-54e2-9b27-ffd3fc3bb110" "answer-e3cf1e9e-5de9-575b-be80-c45bb969b3f6" "answer-55fc80b9-6193-508c-b227-e04b2840c9d6" "answer-e0ded463-bc16-568f-8c0b-62a6554bc47d" "answer-ff980988-6413-52f6-84a6-6d1daa6d7016"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7527daea-f713-54e2-9b27-ffd3fc3bb110" :answer/id #uuid "7527daea-f713-54e2-9b27-ffd3fc3bb110" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{112}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3cf1e9e-5de9-575b-be80-c45bb969b3f6" :answer/id #uuid "e3cf1e9e-5de9-575b-be80-c45bb969b3f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55fc80b9-6193-508c-b227-e04b2840c9d6" :answer/id #uuid "55fc80b9-6193-508c-b227-e04b2840c9d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{51}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0ded463-bc16-568f-8c0b-62a6554bc47d" :answer/id #uuid "e0ded463-bc16-568f-8c0b-62a6554bc47d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff980988-6413-52f6-84a6-6d1daa6d7016" :answer/id #uuid "ff980988-6413-52f6-84a6-6d1daa6d7016" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3412530f-81b3-51bb-95a9-4599a55eb207" :question/id #uuid "3412530f-81b3-51bb-95a9-4599a55eb207" :question/math-academy-id "q-77250" :question/problem "![](images/87/87182d625f4e669a6b2791a17f3e12c34922043b8f3deefd6b2473e8fb529886.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nLet ${C}_{2}$ be the section of parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ from $A$ to $B.$ Then,\n $-{C}_{2}$ will be the same path but travelled from $B$ to $A.$ The path $-{C}_{2}$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i+(4-{t}^{2})\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=4-{t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =x\\,i=t\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-{t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i-2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i)\\cdot (i-2t\\,j) \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2-{\\int }_{0}^{2}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7adcd9af-5265-5496-ada0-3911ecad9cdc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7adcd9af-5265-5496-ada0-3911ecad9cdc" :answer-field/id #uuid "7adcd9af-5265-5496-ada0-3911ecad9cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7e44c518-6f87-55b7-bbc1-4434ed19cb47" :answer-field/choices ["answer-126d13a7-71fa-5051-a4a2-28f29e56cf03" "answer-7aae6b46-36e9-538a-8e22-b5381b749d2c" "answer-7e44c518-6f87-55b7-bbc1-4434ed19cb47" "answer-2216ca5d-6c6a-5107-a068-323114e3fc15" "answer-8ea13c33-b0aa-51a8-a9cd-a56c81fb144a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-126d13a7-71fa-5051-a4a2-28f29e56cf03" :answer/id #uuid "126d13a7-71fa-5051-a4a2-28f29e56cf03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7aae6b46-36e9-538a-8e22-b5381b749d2c" :answer/id #uuid "7aae6b46-36e9-538a-8e22-b5381b749d2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e44c518-6f87-55b7-bbc1-4434ed19cb47" :answer/id #uuid "7e44c518-6f87-55b7-bbc1-4434ed19cb47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2216ca5d-6c6a-5107-a068-323114e3fc15" :answer/id #uuid "2216ca5d-6c6a-5107-a068-323114e3fc15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ea13c33-b0aa-51a8-a9cd-a56c81fb144a" :answer/id #uuid "8ea13c33-b0aa-51a8-a9cd-a56c81fb144a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180722 :tutorial/content "Line integrals of vector-valued functions share a fundamental connection with line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y.$\n\nConsider a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with components $P$ and $Q\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $C$ is a smooth curve on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r (t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [a,b].\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the line integral of $\\mathbf F$ along $C$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(P(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+Q(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}P(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+Q(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y)  \\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nAn analogous result holds for vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3.$ That is, \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d y + R(x,y,z) \\,\\textrm d z\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j + R(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180678 :tutorial/content "Since there is a fundamental link between line integrals of vector-valued functions and line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y,$ it should be unsurprising that they share similar properties. We list two of them below:\n\n- If $-C$ denotes the path obtained by reversing the orientation of $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r = -\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\n- If $C = C_1\\cup C_2$ is the union of two paths $C_1$ and $C_2$, then\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r \n= \n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nThe first property has an intuitive meaning if we think about it in terms of the work done by a force on a particle. To see this, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ shown below. Let $C$ be the path defined by traversing the line segment from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4f/4f69f80d98d301410908f7ec867a89bdc834c22a51f224d9b00b80ceca39ab87.png)\n\n \n\nLet $W(C)$ be the work done in moving the particle along $C.$ Since our particle moves in the same direction as the vector field, we have\n\n$$\nW(C) = \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if we move the particle from $B$ to $A,$ we expect the line integral to be negative. This is because the particle is now moving against $\\mathbf F.$ \n\n![](images/fe/fe6b2e67d33a4993104406c88b1dc0dc897be33d034007ed581a75f741ddf620.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW(-C) = \\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$\n\nWe can show that the work done in moving the particle along $-C$ is the same as the work done in moving it along $C$ but with the opposite sign. That is\n\n$$\nW(-C) = -W(C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r = -\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179857 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
